CORE
nested_call_argument.c
--div-by-zero-check --evaluation-order-check
^\[g\.division-by-zero\.1\] line 10 division by zero in x / y: FAILURE$
^\[main\.evaluation-order\.\d+\] line 16 value of p is independent of evaluation order: FAILURE$
^\[main\.evaluation-order\.\d+\] line 18 value of p is independent of evaluation order: FAILURE$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
The arguments of f are evaluated in an unspecified order, and so are the
operands of nested calls such as g's relative to f's other arguments. When
p-- happens before the call of g, g divides by zero. Moreover, the read of p
as an argument of g is unsequenced relative to p--. Both the declaration with
initializer and the call statement are checked.
